Z||000003||gabriel||04-05-2001||12:12 PM||||&gt;I just put Ryane's tubular front end on my 88GT.Has anyone else used them?<P>  Yes.  I have that item installed on an 85 chassis & had no problems installing it.  I haven't worked on the 88, but I believe that suspension is entirely different from the earier years, & more expensive to get parts for.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||04-05-2001||02:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>I just put Ryane's tubular front end on my 88GT.Has anyone else used them?I can't get the correct camber because the upper control arms won't move towards the chassis enough.The arms have to be unbolted from the chassis and the holes elongated.Has anyone had this problem,and if so ,how did you correct it?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oops. Stupid me. I missed that you have an '88. Ryane/Held only has one tubular setup for '88's that I know of. It doesn't change the geometry, just uses tubular control arms.<BR>The Slalom kit, which does change the front end geometry only fits '84-'87.<P>Exactly which pieces do you have?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||04-05-2001||07:40 PM||||I got the tubular upper and lower control arms and coilovers.Is it possible that one or both of them are for the wrong year car?||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||04-06-2001||09:36 PM||||Anybody?||||reg||1
Z||000007||pherder||04-06-2001||09:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>I got the tubular upper and lower control arms and coilovers.Is it possible that one or both of them are for the wrong year car?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I, too, have the same setup as you do for an 88.<P>I bet you have lowered your car with the coil-over height adjustment, right?<P>If so, you have the same problem that I currently have with my stock setup with The Fiero Store's lowering springs ... you have run out of adjustment as you have already mentioned.<P>The top A-Arm is exactly like the stock one so if the car is lowered, it does the same as the stock one and runs out of adjustment.<P>You will have to do what you already know ... slot the holes so that you can get the necessary adjustment again.<P>Since I hadn't put on my Ryane suspension, yet, but have lowered the stock suspension and have found that little bit of trouble, I had been wondering if the Ryane stuff would correct it ... oh well, I guess elongation of the alignment slots will be necessary anyway I go.<P>Paul<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula<BR>88 Coupe<BR>86 SE<BR>95 GMC<BR>92 Mazda<BR>55 Jones<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||04-06-2001||10:24 PM||||I believe the stock ride height is 5 and 1/2 inches.Mine is set at 6 inches because of the transaxle height.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||04-07-2001||12:00 AM||||Are the upper ball joints the stock ones?<BR>I installed the Fiero Store KYB set-up with Eibach lowering springs. I went with the adjustable ball joints from MOOG. If you have the stock ball joints it will run too close to the edge to get the alignment. I am not sure if the 88's are the same way but on my 86 that's what I needed to do.||||reg||1
